WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:05.400
In this lesson, you'll start
giving your agent more complex tasks.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.433
You will get your agent to parse the job
application form,

3
00:00:08.433 --> 00:00:11.433
convert the parsed form
into a series of simple questions,

4
00:00:11.433 --> 00:00:14.433
and use those questions to query
the RAG pipeline.

5
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:17.300
You'll again use Llama Parse
to extract the fields that need

6
00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:20.300
to be filled before
generating the queries to the RAG system.

7
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.300
Let's get coding. In your previous lesson,

8
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:26.833
you used Llama Parse to parse a resume
and included parsing instructions.

9
00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:28.700
You're going to do that again this time

10
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:30.833
when the instructions
are going to be more advanced.

11
00:00:30.833 --> 00:00:33.133
You're going to get it to read
an application form

12
00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:36.100
and convert it into a list of fields
that need to be filled in,

13
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:39.000
and return that as a Json object.

14
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.800
So let's bring in our imports.

15
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.300
These are all the same imports
that we brought in last time,

16
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:46.100
including our helper functions
to get our API keys.

17
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.133
We're going to need asyncio
just like we did last time.

18
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:50.900
And we're going to fetch

19
00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:53.900
our API keys again,
just like we did last time.

20
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:57.200
Let's execute those three.

21
00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.000
Now we're going to parse a new form.

22
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:01.433
So let's set up our parser first.

23
00:01:01.433 --> 00:01:04.433
Again, we're asking
for a result type of markdown.

24
00:01:04.433 --> 00:01:06.333
But now we're doing two things.

25
00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:09.133
We're providing content guideline
instructions.

26
00:01:09.133 --> 00:01:11.400
We're saying this is a job application
form.

27
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:14.100
Create a list of all the fields
that need to be filled in.

28
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.533
And we're providing
formatting instructions.

29
00:01:16.533 --> 00:01:19.433
Return a bulleted list of the fields only.

30
00:01:19.433 --> 00:01:22.100
This will get us a nice simple result.

31
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:25.200
We can now call load data
on our fake application form.

32
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:28.500
When it's done,
we'll be able to print out the results.

33
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.300
As you can see, it
follows the instructions exactly.

34
00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:35.700
We have a bulleted list

35
00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:39.500
of just the fields in the form.
A useful thing that LLMs can do

36
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:43.433
is turn human-readable formats
like this list into machine-readable ones.

37
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:44.900
We're going to do that here

38
00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:48.700
by asking it to turn the list into a Json
object with an array of fields.

39
00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:51.133
To do that, we'll need an LLM.

40
00:01:51.133 --> 00:01:52.500
So let's bring in OpenAI.

41
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:54.400
We're going to

42
00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.733
give our LLM a prompt
and our list of fields.

43
00:01:58.333 --> 00:02:00.233
We're going to say this is a parsed form.

44
00:02:00.233 --> 00:02:02.433
Here's the data and return Json

45
00:02:02.433 --> 00:02:07.233
only. When we execute that will be able
to print out the raw Json

46
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.100
and see what we got.

47
00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:12.100
You can see it's given us Json.

48
00:02:12.233 --> 00:02:17.233
So now we can parse the Json and print out
the list of fields programmatically.

49
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:19.100
Perfect.

50
00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:19.633
We've now

51
00:02:19.633 --> 00:02:23.800
got a nice array of fields that we can use
for all of our programmatic work later.

52
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.733
So now let's take the workflow you built
in the last lesson and add your parser.

53
00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:33.300
We're going to need two new events
the parse form event and the query event.

54
00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.833
And here is your new workflow.

55
00:02:36.833 --> 00:02:37.900
It's a lot longer.

56
00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:40.133
So we're going to step through it
line by line.

57
00:02:40.133 --> 00:02:42.900
Just like before
you've got a RAG workflow class

58
00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.633
with a storage directory
an LLM and a query engine. In our setup,

59
00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:51.200
we're now going to emit a parse form event
which triggers your new step, parse form.

60
00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.300
So here you see
parse form event happening.

61
00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.533
And here's our new method, parse form.

62
00:02:56.533 --> 00:03:01.200
This is the code that we just wrote above
incorporated into our workflow.

63
00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:03.000
There's our parser.

64
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.200
There's this converting it into Json.

65
00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:06.133
And there's this

66
00:03:06.133 --> 00:03:07.633
printing out our Json.

67
00:03:07.633 --> 00:03:09.833
We're going to leave
the rest of the workflow untouched

68
00:03:09.833 --> 00:03:11.400
and handle the rest of it later.

69
00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.233
So let's execute this to get it going.

70
00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:15.933
And now

71
00:03:15.933 --> 00:03:18.933
let's execute the workflow.

72
00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:19.900
Great.

73
00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:21.433
It did exactly the same thing

74
00:03:21.433 --> 00:03:24.433
the code outside of the workflow did,
which is all we needed it to do.

75
00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:29.600
So now your workflow knows what fields it
needs answers for. In the next iteration,

76
00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:30.833
you can fire off one

77
00:03:30.833 --> 00:03:34.900
query event for each of the fields,
and they'll be executed concurrently.

78
00:03:35.033 --> 00:03:38.300
You'll remember in lesson two
we talked about doing concurrent steps.

79
00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:41.100
The changes you're going to make
are: you're going to generate

80
00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:44.233
a query event for each of the questions
you pulled out of the form.

81
00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:46.800
You're going to create a fill
in application step,

82
00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.300
which will take all of the responses
to the questions

83
00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:51.700
and aggregate them
into a coherent response.

84
00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:55.400
And you're going to add a response event
to pass the results of queries

85
00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:56.933
to fill in application.

86
00:03:56.933 --> 00:03:59.233
So let's see what that looks like.

87
00:03:59.233 --> 00:04:00.333
Here are events.

88
00:04:00.333 --> 00:04:02.500
There's a new one, response event.

89
00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:03.600
And here's our workflow.

90
00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:06.400
As we saw

91
00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:09.400
earlier, we have a parse form event.

92
00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.500
And now we have a new step.

93
00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:15.700
Instead of just printing out the fields,
for each of the fields,

94
00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:18.700
we're going to send an event,
specifically a query event.

95
00:04:18.933 --> 00:04:22.800
The query event will accept the field
that we're talking about and a query.

96
00:04:23.133 --> 00:04:24.800
It's going to be a pretty simple query.

97
00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:27.800
Just, how would you answer this question
about the candidate?

98
00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.133
The other thing we're going to do is

99
00:04:29.133 --> 00:04:32.633
we're going to store the number of fields,
so we know how many to wait for later.

100
00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:36.500
The next thing that will happen
is that the ask

101
00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:40.500
questions step will be fired off
as many times as we had query events.

102
00:04:40.533 --> 00:04:42.933
Each of them
is going to return a response event,

103
00:04:42.933 --> 00:04:45.600
which is going to trigger
the fill-in application step.

104
00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:46.300
The response

105
00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:50.300
event will run into our collect events
method that we talked about earlier.

106
00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.033
In this case, we're going to wait
for a number of response event

107
00:04:54.033 --> 00:04:57.233
equivalent to the number of total fields
that we saw earlier.

108
00:04:57.500 --> 00:04:59.500
Remember
we're storing this in the context.

109
00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:00.433
So it's available

110
00:05:00.433 --> 00:05:04.500
across steps even though we haven't passed
it using an event. As before,

111
00:05:04.733 --> 00:05:07.200
if collect events returns nothing, we'll do

112
00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:07.700
nothing.

113
00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:11.833
But once we've got all of the responses,
we'll turn them into a list that we can

114
00:05:11.833 --> 00:05:14.833
then pass into our LLM
with another prompt.

115
00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:18.200
In this case, we're going to ask it
to answer the questions.

116
00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:20.600
Then we're going to emit a stop event.

117
00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:22.733
So now let's execute that workflow.

118
00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:25.933
Just like before,

119
00:05:25.933 --> 00:05:28.933
we're passing in a fake resume
and a fake application form.

120
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.500
And we're printing our result.

121
00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:34.300
It might take a little while
because it has to ask a bunch of questions

122
00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:37.300
this time.

123
00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:39.400
Great.

124
00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:42.100
As you can see,
the LLM has helpfully explained what

125
00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:45.800
it's done and given us a numbered list
of all of the fields and their answers.

126
00:05:46.133 --> 00:05:47.100
Your workflow takes

127
00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:50.600
all the fields in the form and generates
plausible answers to all of them.

128
00:05:51.133 --> 00:05:53.900
There are a couple of fields
where I think it can do better.

129
00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:54.700
Portfolio

130
00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:58.400
for instance. In the original document,
the portfolio has a link,

131
00:05:58.700 --> 00:06:01.333
whereas here it's trying to list out
a portfolio.

132
00:06:01.333 --> 00:06:04.333
We'll use our feedback
to see if we can correct that.

133
00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:08.233
In the next lesson, you'll add the ability
to give that feedback to the agent

134
00:06:08.333 --> 00:06:09.500
and get it to try again.

